    ONE PROCESS PER INTERFACE (issue 124).

    synapse-sensor captures exactly one device per process, so this model is a
    LIST of capture instances plus one block of settings they share. Each
    instance becomes its own synapse-sensor process, with its own sensor
    identity, its own rendered configuration, its own pidfile and its own log.

    That shape was chosen over merging several interfaces into one process
    because it gives correct attribution with no protocol change: a packet
    routed between two monitored segments is legitimately observed twice, by two
    NAMED sensors, instead of two observations silently collapsing into one flow.
    See docs/adr/0031-opnsense-one-sensor-process-per-interface.md.

      general    - the transport posture and the secrets. One collector, one
                   bearer token, one TLS identity for this firewall.
      instances  - what to capture, once per interface, and WHO SAYS SO: the
                   authorisation assertion is per instance (PROJECT.md 28.18),
                   because capturing a new segment is a new decision that cannot
                   be inherited from another instance.

    NOTE: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen, so command line flags
    are written below without their leading dashes: "iface" means the `iface`
    flag, "token-file" means the `token-file` flag, and so on.

    Two settings deliberately do not become flag *values*:

      * "token" is NEVER turned into a command line argument. It is rendered
        into /usr/local/etc/synapseids/sensor.token (0400 _synapseids) and
        passed by path with the `token-file` flag, so it can never show up in
        ps(1).
      * "ca" / "client_cert" / "client_key" hold PEM *material*; configd renders
        them into files with restrictive modes and only the resulting paths
        reach the command line.
-->
<model>
    <mount>//OPNsense/SynapseIDSSensor</mount>
    <version>1.0.1</version>
    <description>
        SynapseIDS distributed capture sensors (synapse-sensor pcap-over-ip),
        one process per captured interface.
    </description>
    <items>
        <general>
            <!-- ============================= general ======================= -->
            <!--
                Master switch. Individual instances have their own enable flag;
                this one turns the whole plugin off without disturbing them.
            -->
            <enabled type="BooleanField">
                <default>0</default>
                <Required>Y</Required>
            </enabled>

            <!-- ============================ transport ====================== -->
            <!--
                Shared by every instance: there is one SynapseIDS collector and
                one firewall, so a per-instance collector address, token or TLS
                identity would multiply the secrets on the box without buying
                anything. The one transport setting that CANNOT be shared is the
                listen address, because four processes cannot bind one port -
                that lives on the instance.
            -->
            <!-- Selects between the mutually exclusive `listen` and `connect` flags. -->
            <mode type="OptionField">
                <default>listen</default>
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <OptionValues>
                    <listen>Listen - the SynapseIDS daemon connects to this firewall</listen>
                    <connect>Connect - this firewall connects out to the SynapseIDS daemon</connect>
                </OptionValues>
                <ValidationMessage>Choose listen or connect.</ValidationMessage>
            </mode>

            <!-- mode = connect: value of the `connect` flag, shared by every instance. -->
            <address type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
                <Mask>/^(|([a-zA-Z0-9]([a-zA-Z0-9._-]*[a-zA-Z0-9])?|\[[0-9a-fA-F:.]+\]):[0-9]{1,5})$/</Mask>
                <ValidationMessage>Use host:port of the SynapseIDS collector, for example ids.example.net:4789.</ValidationMessage>
            </address>

            <!-- ============================== identity ===================== -->
            <!--
                Shared bearer token. Stored in config.xml and written to
                sensor.token only. Space and quote characters are refused so the
                value can never disturb the generated POSIX-sh configuration.
            -->
            <token type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
                <Mask>/^[!-~]{0,512}$/</Mask>
                <ValidationMessage>The token must be printable ASCII without spaces (max 512 characters).</ValidationMessage>
            </token>

            <!-- ================================ TLS ======================== -->
            <!--
                connect mode only: when unchecked every instance is started with
                the `insecure-tls` flag, which the binary itself refuses unless
                `authorized` is also given (see Sensor::performValidation).
            -->
            <verify_peer type="BooleanField">
                <default>1</default>
                <Required>Y</Required>
            </verify_peer>

            <!--
                All three fields below hold PEM *text*. Since issue #104 configd
                renders each to its own file and only the resulting path reaches
                the command line:

                  ca          -> /usr/local/etc/synapseids/sensor-ca.pem    0444 root:wheel
                  client_cert -> /usr/local/etc/synapseids/sensor-cert.pem  0444 root:wheel
                  client_key  -> /usr/local/etc/synapseids/sensor-key.pem   0400 _synapseids

                A Mask cannot express a multi-line PEM blob usefully (and a
                regular expression is the wrong tool for "is this a certificate"),
                so all three are validated in Sensor::performValidation instead:
                complete BEGIN/END blocks, decodable base64, the right block type
                for the field, a key that OpenSSL can actually parse, no
                passphrase-protected key, and the certificate and key proven to
                match each other. A blob that fails any of those is a save-time
                configuration error, not a start-time surprise on a remote
                firewall.
            -->

            <!--
                Peer verification bundle.
                  listen  mode : becomes the `client-ca` flag (require and
                                 verify a client certificate)
                  connect mode : becomes the `ca` flag (verify the collector)
                One or more CERTIFICATE blocks; a private key here is refused.
            -->
            <ca type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
                <Multiline>Y</Multiline>
                <ValidationMessage>Paste one or more PEM CERTIFICATE blocks (a CA bundle), not a private key.</ValidationMessage>
            </ca>

            <!--
                This firewall's own key pair, becoming the `cert` and `key`
                flags on every instance.
                  listen  mode : the TLS server identity
                  connect mode : the mutual TLS client identity
                Both or neither, enforced in performValidation().
            -->
            <client_cert type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
                <Multiline>Y</Multiline>
                <ValidationMessage>Paste a single PEM CERTIFICATE block. Put any intermediates in the CA field.</ValidationMessage>
            </client_cert>

            <!--
                The private key. This is the second secret on the firewall after
                the bearer token and it is treated the same way: rendered only to
                a 0400 _synapseids file, clamped by the fixperms configd action
                immediately after every template reload, re-checked by the rc.d
                script before every start, and never written to any sensor.conf,
                to argv or to a log.
            -->
            <client_key type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
                <Multiline>Y</Multiline>
                <ValidationMessage>Paste an unencrypted PEM PRIVATE KEY block matching the certificate above.</ValidationMessage>
            </client_key>

            <!-- ====================== deprecated, model 1.0.0 ============== -->
            <!--
                THESE TEN FIELDS ARE NOT SHOWN ANYWHERE AND ARE NOT USED AT RUN
                TIME. They are the model 1.0.0 single-instance settings, kept
                declared for exactly one reason: Migrations/M1_0_1.php reads them
                to build the first instance, and a model migration can only read
                what the model declares. Losing an operator's working sensor on
                upgrade is not an acceptable outcome, and reaching around the
                model into config.xml to avoid ten dead leaves would be a much
                bigger assumption than leaving them here.

                All are TextField/Required=N with no default and no Mask, so that
                whatever 1.0.0 stored - including the "wan,opt5,opt4,opt2" that a
                pre issue-132 multi-select could produce - loads without a
                validation error and can be migrated. M1_0_1 blanks them once it
                has copied them, so a migrated configuration carries no duplicate
                of its own settings.
            -->
            <interface type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </interface>
            <filter type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </filter>
            <direction type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </direction>
            <promiscuous type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </promiscuous>
            <snaplen type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </snaplen>
            <send_mode type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </send_mode>
            <listen_address type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </listen_address>
            <sensor_id type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </sensor_id>
            <location type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </location>
            <authorized type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </authorized>
        </general>

        <!-- ============================== instances ======================== -->
        <!--
            One repeating item per capture interface, rendered as a grid on
            Services then SynapseIDS Sensor. ArrayField is the core idiom for a
            list of things (System then Settings then Cron is the reference
            implementation: OPNsense/Cron/Cron.xml declares `job` exactly this
            way), so nothing here is invented.

            Each item becomes:
              * one rc.d profile name, listed in synapseids_sensor_profiles
              * one rendered /usr/local/etc/synapseids/instances/NAME.conf
              * one pidfile  /var/run/synapseids/NAME.pid
              * one log      /var/log/synapseids/NAME/sensor.log
              * one line per check in `service synapseids_sensor selftest`
        -->
        <instances>
            <instance type="ArrayField">
                <!--
                    Per-instance enable. The plugin-wide switch is
                    general.enabled; this one takes a single segment out of
                    service without deleting its configuration.
                -->
                <enabled type="BooleanField">
                    <default>1</default>
                    <Required>Y</Required>
                </enabled>

                <!--
                    The instance name is not decoration: it is the FreeBSD rc
                    profile name (synapseids_sensor_profiles), the name of the
                    rendered configuration file, the pidfile stem, the log
                    directory and the column the selftest prints. So it is
                    restricted to what is simultaneously a safe filename and a
                    safe shell variable fragment - letters, digits, underscore.
                    A dash would break `synapseids_sensor_NAME_...` if this ever
                    needs per-profile rc variables, so it is excluded now rather
                    than after somebody hits it.
                -->
                <name type="TextField">
                    <Required>Y</Required>
                    <Mask>/^[a-zA-Z0-9_]{1,32}$/</Mask>
                    <ValidationMessage>Use 1 to 32 letters, digits or underscores, unique across instances (it names the service profile, its log directory and its configuration file).</ValidationMessage>
                </name>

                <!--
                    OPNsense interface *identifier* (wan, lan, opt1, ...), single
                    select. One process captures one device.

                    This was a multi-select in model 1.0.0 while the configd
                    template resolved only the first identifier, which on a live
                    gateway meant an operator selected WAN, IoT, DMZ and MGMT and
                    got one VLAN: four segments believed monitored, one actually
                    monitored, and nothing anywhere reporting the difference
                    (issue 124). The answer is one instance per interface, not a
                    control that takes input and discards it.
                -->
                <interface type="InterfaceField">
                    <Required>N</Required>
                    <ValidationMessage>Select the single interface this sensor captures from.</ValidationMessage>
                </interface>

                <!--
                    listen mode only: the address THIS instance binds. It has to
                    be per instance because four processes cannot share one port;
                    performValidation refuses duplicates among enabled instances,
                    since the second process to start would simply die with
                    "address already in use" and the segment would go unmonitored.
                    Ignored in connect mode, where every instance dials the one
                    shared collector address.
                -->
                <listen_address type="TextField">
                    <default>0.0.0.0:4789</default>
                    <Required>N</Required>
                    <Mask>/^(|(([0-9]{1,3}\.){3}[0-9]{1,3}|\[[0-9a-fA-F:.]+\])?:[0-9]{1,5})$/</Mask>
                    <ValidationMessage>Use host:port, for example 0.0.0.0:4789, :4789 or [::]:4789. Each enabled instance needs its own port.</ValidationMessage>
                </listen_address>

                <!-- ========================= capture ======================= -->
                <!--
                    Maps to the `filter` flag. An empty value means "no filter at
                    all" and the flag is omitted entirely.

                    An empty OptionValues key cannot be expressed in model XML (an
                    XML tag cannot be named ""), so the empty "All traffic" choice
                    uses Required=N plus BlankDesc, which is the supported core
                    idiom: BaseListField, the parent of OptionField, offers a blank
                    entry described by BlankDesc precisely when the field is not
                    required.

                    Worst case if that is wrong on some core: the dropdown simply
                    does not offer the blank entry, so an operator cannot select
                    "capture everything" and is limited to the four presets. That is
                    a cosmetic limitation, visible immediately in the GUI, and it
                    cannot produce a dead or misconfigured sensor.

                    The rendered value is validated twice more anyway: the OptionField
                    itself refuses a value outside this list, and `synapse-sensor
                    doctor` fails its config check if a rendered configuration ever
                    names a filter that is not a built-in preset.
                -->
                <filter type="OptionField">
                    <default>ip-any</default>
                    <Required>N</Required>
                    <BlankDesc>All traffic</BlankDesc>
                    <OptionValues>
                        <ip>IPv4 only</ip>
                        <ip6>IPv6 only</ip6>
                        <ip-any>IPv4 + IPv6</ip-any>
                        <not-arp>Everything except ARP</not-arp>
                    </OptionValues>
                    <ValidationMessage>Choose one of the built-in capture filter presets.</ValidationMessage>
                </filter>

                <!-- Maps to the `direction` flag; valid only together with `iface`. -->
                <direction type="OptionField">
                    <default>in</default>
                    <Required>Y</Required>
                    <OptionValues>
                        <inout>Both directions</inout>
                        <in>Inbound only</in>
                        <out>Outbound only</out>
                    </OptionValues>
                    <ValidationMessage>Choose inbound, outbound or both.</ValidationMessage>
                </direction>

                <!-- Maps to the boolean `promisc` flag. -->
                <promiscuous type="BooleanField">
                    <default>1</default>
                    <Required>Y</Required>
                </promiscuous>

                <!-- Maps to the `snaplen` flag. -->
                <snaplen type="IntegerField">
                    <default>262144</default>
                    <Required>Y</Required>
                    <MinimumValue>64</MinimumValue>
                    <MaximumValue>262144</MaximumValue>
                    <ValidationMessage>Snap length must be between 64 and 262144 bytes.</ValidationMessage>
                </snaplen>

                <!-- ======================= what to send ==================== -->
                <!--
                    Maps to the `mode` flag: how much of the pipeline runs here on
                    the firewall, and therefore how much crosses the WAN link.

                      raw     - stream every captured frame. Most bandwidth, and the
                                packet content leaves this host.
                      flow    - aggregate flows here and send one flow record per
                                closed flow. Measured at roughly 1.5 percent of the
                                raw bandwidth on ordinary traffic.
                      feature - also compute the 48 flow-features-v1 values here and
                                send only those. NO packet content ever leaves this
                                host, which is the point of the mode; roughly 1.9
                                percent of the raw bandwidth.

                    Per instance, deliberately: a WAN uplink and an internal SPAN
                    port rarely warrant the same bandwidth or the same privacy
                    posture. The verdicts are identical in all three modes.

                    Note the name: the transport posture field in `general` is
                    already called "mode" (listen versus connect), so this one is
                    "send_mode" even though it renders as the `mode` flag.

                    The record modes need a SynapseIDS daemon new enough to speak
                    SYNPOIP v2. An older collector refuses the connection with
                    "mode-unsupported" rather than quietly falling back to sending
                    packets, so an operator who chose "feature" for privacy reasons
                    never silently loses that property.
                -->
                <send_mode type="OptionField">
                    <default>raw</default>
                    <Required>Y</Required>
                    <OptionValues>
                        <raw>Raw packets - stream every frame (most bandwidth)</raw>
                        <flow>Flow records - aggregate flows here (least bandwidth)</flow>
                        <feature>Feature vectors only - no packet content leaves this host</feature>
                    </OptionValues>
                    <ValidationMessage>Choose raw, flow or feature.</ValidationMessage>
                </send_mode>

                <!-- ========================= identity ====================== -->
                <!--
                    Maps to the `sensor-id` flag. This is the whole reason for
                    running a process per interface: the daemon attributes every
                    flow to the sensor that saw it, so two instances must never
                    share an id. performValidation refuses duplicates.
                -->
                <sensor_id type="TextField">
                    <Required>N</Required>
                    <Mask>/^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]{0,64}$/</Mask>
                    <ValidationMessage>Sensor ID may contain letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore (max 64), and must be unique across instances.</ValidationMessage>
                </sensor_id>

                <!-- Maps to the `location` flag. -->
                <location type="TextField">
                    <Required>N</Required>
                    <Mask>/^[a-zA-Z0-9 ._\/-]{0,64}$/</Mask>
                    <ValidationMessage>Location may contain letters, digits, space, dot, slash, dash and underscore (max 64).</ValidationMessage>
                </location>

                <!-- ======================= authorization =================== -->
                <!--
                    PROJECT.md 28.18: capturing live traffic is an explicit
                    authorization decision, never a default - and it is PER
                    INSTANCE. Being authorised to monitor the WAN uplink says
                    nothing about being authorised to monitor a tenant VLAN, so
                    this assertion is never inherited from another instance and
                    is never copied when a new instance is added.

                    Becomes the `authorized` flag, which synapse-sensor requires
                    whenever the `iface` flag is used.
                -->
                <authorized type="BooleanField">
                    <default>0</default>
                    <Required>Y</Required>
                </authorized>

                <description type="TextField">
                    <Required>N</Required>
                    <Mask>/^[a-zA-Z0-9 ._\/,()-]{0,128}$/</Mask>
                    <ValidationMessage>Free text: letters, digits, space and . _ / , ( ) - (max 128).</ValidationMessage>
                </description>
            </instance>
        </instances>
    </items>
</model>
